1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,440
 Now we'll look at another AWS security service called as AWS Identity and Access Management.

2
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:19,920
 In short, it's called as IAM. Now, AWS IM service securely controls access to AWS resources.

3
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:26,640
 When we say AWS resources, we mean any AWS servers such as S3, EC2, DynamoDB, and so on.

4
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:34,400
 Now, IAM service focuses on two areas. One, it authenticates the user and that could be through

5
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:40,880
 AWS console with username, password, or programmatically through secret key and access key.

6
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:48,080
 And the second thing it focuses on is authorization. What is the level of permission

7
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:54,240
 that the authenticated user has? Does it have access to all the services or does it have access

8
00:00:54,240 --> 00:01:01,440
 to, let's say, only one service S3 bucket? Now, within AWS IM, there are three key concepts

9
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:08,160
 that you need to know. One is the AWS IM users and groups. IAM rules, IAM policies, and permissions.

10
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:17,120
 Now, let's take a look at IAM users and groups. So, IAM users are assigned to workforce users.

11
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:26,560
 Users are created for individuals who need access to AWS services. So, let's say here,

12
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:33,520
 IAM Rahul and IAM a server admin. So, if I need to access this EC2 instance or this S3 bucket,

13
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:39,920
 we'll need to create an IAM user who will have access to these servers. Next, let's say we have

14
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:46,720
 Joe who is the billing admin. So, he will need access to the billing console. So, we'll create

15
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:52,320
 a user within the identity and access management service for Joe who will have access to the

16
00:01:52,320 --> 00:02:00,240
 to the billing service. So, that is what IAM user is used for. So, once you have created the IAM user,

17
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:06,160
 then you will need to attach policy and we'll get to what are policies. But just think about

18
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:14,000
 policies as permission level. So, whether Rahul needs access to only EC2 instance S3 or both of them,

19
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,160
 it will give it the right permission level. Then, IAM users and groups are sometimes called

20
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:28,720
 as identities with long-term credentials and what that means is. Now, let's say IAM Rahul,

21
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:34,960
 the server admin. So, I will get a user name and password if I want to access the AWS account.

22
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:38,800
 So, it will be a long long-term credential. So, it can be there for weeks, months,

23
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:44,720
 or till the time I am in the project. And as we will see later, there are also some temporary

24
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:51,920
 security credentials. But IAM user and groups provide long-term credentials. So, now let's

25
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:58,320
 understand what groups mean. Now, within the organization, you could have a lot of different team

26
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:05,680
 members who will act as server admins. So, rather than creating multiple users and giving them

27
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:11,360
 similar permissions, you could create a group, maybe you create a server admin group,

28
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:18,000
 and then just add users to that group. So, let me just quickly show you. I am on the AWS

