00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:11,040
 add the users within this user group. Now, the next concept within IM is IM roles. So, unlike the

47
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:18,960
 users where you will have the credentials to access the console or programmatically access

48
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:28,000
 various services, IM role is assumed by an AWS service or an application. Now, let us

49
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,760
 understand what that means. So, let us say we have this EC2 instance. And this is the example

50
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:38,960
 we had discussed earlier, we said it acts as a web server and it is going to fetch the images

51
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:48,400
 from the S3 bucket. Now, by default, no AWS service has access to another service. And that is

52
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:55,920
 important because let us say if this EC2 instance had access to all the S3 buckets within your account,

53
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:01,360
 then in some of the S3 buckets you might be storing confidential information. So, someone

54
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:08,160
 let us say Rahul who has access to this EC2 instance can go and fetch data from that S3 bucket.

55
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:15,760
 So, that is why every time you are creating an application, you need to create a role for this

56
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:24,160
 EC2 instance which allows it access to maybe S3 bucket or if it needs to access, maybe an RDS

57
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:33,280
 database or any other service. So, in most occasions when you want to provide access to an AWS

58
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:41,360
 service like EC2, S3 and so on to another service, then it is used something known as IM role.

59
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,600
 So, you create an IM role just like the user and then you attach a set of permissions

60
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:56,560
 which allows it access to another AWS service. And this is sometimes called as temporary

61
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:04,720
 security credentials because the access that service has is valid only for limited duration,

62
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:11,520
 maybe R or a couple of hours. So, hopefully it is clear, I am users and groups are used for work

63
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:19,440
 for users, IM roles are used for AWS services and applications. But please note in very rare

64
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:25,120
 circumstances when you are providing cross account access, which means let us say you have 10

65
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:32,720
 accounts with no organization and one of the users or workforce users has access to a particular

66
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:38,480
 account. But let us say he needs to access the billing details of the second account.

67
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:45,920
 In that scenario, you will attach an IM role to this user but that is a very rare scenario.

68
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:51,280
 In most scenarios, you can assume if it is an individual or a human, you can create an IM

69
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:59,360
 user or group for them, if it is an AWS service or an application, then you can create an

70
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:08,720
 IM role with the exception of cross account access, then we have the IM permission and policies.

71
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:16,560
 So, permissions define which actions are allowed or denied. So, let us say there is an

72
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:22,000
 AWS service that is trying to access this S3 bucket. So, it can have read-only access,

73
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:30,400
 create access, delete access or all three. So, the permissions define what actions are allowed or

74
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:39,120
 denied. And then we have the IM policy, it is basically a document that defines the permissions

75
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:47,040
 that can be granted to an IM role or IM user and group. So, what you essentially do is,

76
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:55,680
 once you create either an IM user, group or a role, you attach the policies to the IM role

77
00:08:55,680 --> 00:09:01,200
 or IM group and user. That is pretty much it from this lecture. I will see you in the next

